module fixtures.dag_artifact_identity.perturbed_registry_order_specimen

// THE FIXTURE THIS CONTROL NEEDS IS A MODULE WITH MANY ITEM-REGISTRY KEYS, AND THE COUNT IS THE
// WHOLE POINT. `dag-artifact.json` carries `item_registry_keys` per module and once for the
// graph; the defect this specimen discriminates emitted that SET in host map order, so two runs
// of one binary over one tree agreed on every `$ref` and disagreed on those lists. A two-key
// module would agree by chance half the time, which is a control that reads green on a tree that
// still carries the defect. Sixteen declarations make a chance agreement 1/16! -- still not zero,
// and stated rather than rounded to "deterministic".
//
// The declarations are deliberately trivial and mutually independent: the subject is the
// EMITTER'S key ordering, so anything the resolver could reorder for its own reasons would
// confound it.
//
// THIS IS THE PERTURBED ARM: the subject specimen plus exactly one declaration, `item_quebec`.
// It exists so the equality half of the control cannot pass by comparing nothing -- an emitter
// that emitted the same bytes for every input would satisfy equality perfectly.
//
// WHAT THE BYTE DIFFERENCE ALONE DOES NOT ESTABLISH, stated here because the first version of
// this control claimed it (review 64007). These are two FILES, so they differ in module name as
// well as in the declaration, and `serialize_node_record` emits names and spans -- so the two
// artifacts would differ even if `item_quebec` never reached the emitted bytes at all. The
// control therefore NAMES the declaration and checks both directions of its membership
// (`DAG_ARTIFACT_IDENTITY_ADDED_DECLARATION`), rather than inferring it from inequality. Keep
// the arms one declaration apart: that is what those two assertions are about.

fn item_alpha() -> Int { 1 }
fn item_bravo() -> Int { 2 }
fn item_charlie() -> Int { 3 }
fn item_delta() -> Int { 4 }
fn item_echo() -> Int { 5 }
fn item_foxtrot() -> Int { 6 }
fn item_golf() -> Int { 7 }
fn item_hotel() -> Int { 8 }
fn item_india() -> Int { 9 }
fn item_juliett() -> Int { 10 }
fn item_kilo() -> Int { 11 }
fn item_lima() -> Int { 12 }
fn item_mike() -> Int { 13 }
fn item_november() -> Int { 14 }
fn item_oscar() -> Int { 15 }
fn item_papa() -> Int { 16 }

fn main() -> Int { item_alpha() }

fn item_quebec() -> Int { 17 }
